Here’s a complete .gitignore template for Unity projects, based on the official GitHub Unity .gitignore and best practices.
This will help you keep your repository clean by ignoring generated files, caches, and local settings.

Gitignore# =============== #
# Unity .gitignore
# =============== #

# OS-specific files
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# User-specific files
*.user
*.userprefs
*.csproj
*.pidb
*.suo
*.sln
*.tmp
*.svd
*.pdb
*.mdb
*.opendb
*.VC.db

# Unity generated folders
Library/
Temp/
Obj/
Build/
Builds/
Logs/
MemoryCaptures/
UserSettings/

# Asset meta data should only be ignored when the corresponding asset is also ignored
# *.meta

# Autogenerated Jetbrains Rider plugin
.idea/
*.iml

# Visual Studio cache directory
.vs/

# Gradle cache (for Android builds)
.gradle/
gradle/

# Android build artifacts
*.apk
*.aab
*.unitypackage

# Crash reports
sysinfo.txt

# Addressables
/[Aa]ssets/[Aa]ddressable[Aa]ssets[Dd]ata/*/*.bin*
/[Aa]ssets/[Aa]ddressable[Aa]ssets[Dd]ata/*/*.hash

# Unity Collaborate
Collab/

# Cache for Unity Asset Store
[Aa]ssets/AssetStoreTools*

# Temporary auto-generated Android keystore
*.keystore
*.jks

# Rider plugin
.idea/

# VS Code settings
.vscode/

# Build artifacts
[Bb]uild/
[Bb]uilds/


📌 How to use:

Create a file named .gitignore in the root of your Unity project.
Paste the above content into it.
Commit the .gitignore before committing other files to ensure unwanted files are never tracked.


💡 Tip:
If you already committed unwanted files before adding .gitignore, run:
Bashgit rm -r --cached .
git add .
git commit -m "Apply Unity .gitignore"

This will remove them from Git history while keeping them locally.

